Subject: Skip the empty register liveness bitmap walk

What this does
  Skips the block liveness extension when a function has no register values.
  This avoids forming pointers from absent bitmaps.

Why we need it
  The register allocator does not allocate liveness bitmaps when a function has
  no SSA values. The later interval extension still formed pointers from those
  null bitmaps, which UBSan reports as undefined behavior.

What it adds
  An empty-value guard on the block walk.

Needs a bytecode version bump
  No.

--- a/quickjs.c
+++ b/quickjs.c
@@ -57756,17 +57756,12 @@ static __exception int js_regvm_build_cfg(JSContext *ctx, JSFunctionDef *fd,
             iend[i] = (istart[i] < n_lin) ? e : -1;
         }
 
-        /*
-         * A live range can wrap. When a loop's back edge runs from a block laid
-         * out before the block that defines the value -- an enumerator carried
-         * round, a phi input from an earlier block -- the value is live in a
-         * block whose instructions come earlier in linear order than its own
-         * definition. An interval that ran only from the definition to the last
-         * use would then have both ends on one side of the loop body, and the
-         * body would be free to take the register. So every value's interval is
-         * grown to cover each block it is live in, start as well as end.
-         */
-        for (b = 0; b < n_blocks; b++) {
+        /* A live range can wrap: a back edge from a block laid out before the
+           defining block keeps the value live in earlier blocks. Each interval
+           is grown to cover every block it is live in, start as well as end. */
+        /* No bitsets without register values; even a zero offset from a null
+           pointer is undefined behavior. */
+        for (b = 0; words > 0 && b < n_blocks; b++) {
             uint64_t *bin, *bout;
             if (in_depth[b] < 0)
                 continue;
